MOSAIC: A Universal Architecture for Vehicle Autonomy

Mathematical Foundations, Formal Safety, and Technical Specification

ouadi Maakoul 

AI : chatGpt, Grok XAi, Deepseek, Meta AI

Abstract

This thesis presents MOSAIC (Modular Open Safety Architecture for Intelligent Control), a universal architecture for vehicle autonomy that rigorously separates statistical intelligence from deterministic safety certification. The architecture is organized into three domains—Intelligence, Safety, and Vehicle—connected by the Universal Vehicle Abstraction Layer (UVAL), a formally specified typed interface with mathematical pre- and postconditions. The safety-critical core is a 100 Hz Safety Kernel that filters AI-generated commands through a high-order control barrier function quadratic program (HOCBF-QP). We develop a mathematically rigorous safety filter for the kinematic bicycle model with curvature-based virtual input, which eliminates the singularity of yaw-rate formulations at zero speed and renders the HOCBF constraints affine in the control. Collision, lane, speed, and tire-friction barriers are derived with explicit relative degrees. A moving-obstacle barrier is robustified against bounded obstacle acceleration and ego-model disturbances via input-to-state safe HOCBF (ISSf-HOCBF) conditions, with correct worst-case signs and dimensionally consistent margins. A recursive feasibility layer constructs a robust controllable subset of the safe set in which the QP is provably feasible at every control cycle, strengthening the classical fallback theorem. The architecture is designed to ASIL-D principles and incorporates a dual-channel mechanical bypass for retrofit vehicles. We provide a complete UVAL contract including EV-specific energy and regenerative-braking primitives, a fixed-iteration active-set solver with proven WCET bounds, and a falsifiable experimental protocol on a Dacia Spring platform. The result is a certifiable, manufacturer-independent substrate for limited-domain autonomous driving.

