Part I — Mathematical Foundations

Chapter 1 — Dynamical Systems, Safety, and Invariance

1.1 Control-Affine Systems with Disturbances

We consider control-affine nonlinear systems of the form

\dot{\mathbf{x}} = f(\mathbf{x}) + g(\mathbf{x})\mathbf{u} + g_d(\mathbf{x})d(t),
\tag{1.1}

where $\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{X}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^n$ is the state, $\mathbf{u}\in\mathcal{U}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^m$ is the control input, $d(t)\in\mathbb{R}^p$ is a disturbance with $\|d(t)\|\le \bar d$, and $f$, $g$, $g_d$ are locally Lipschitz continuous.

Assumption 1.1 (Existence and uniqueness). For every $\mathbf{x}(0)\in\mathcal{X}$ and every locally Lipschitz control $\mathbf{u}(\cdot)$, the solution $\mathbf{x}(t)$ of (1.1) exists and is unique on some interval $[0,T)$.

Assumption 1.2 (Bounded disturbance derivatives). The disturbance $d(\cdot)$ is $r{-}1$ times differentiable with $\|d^{(i)}(t)\|\le \bar d_i$ for $i=0,\dots,r{-}1$, where $r$ is the highest relative degree of any barrier considered.

1.2 Safe Sets and Forward Invariance

Definition 1.1 (Safe set). A set $\mathcal{C}\subseteq\mathcal{X}$ is a safe set if it is the zero-superlevel set of a continuously differentiable function $h:\mathbb{R}^n\to\mathbb{R}$:

\mathcal{C} = \{\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{X} : h(\mathbf{x})\ge 0\},
\tag{1.2}

with boundary $\partial\mathcal{C} = \{\mathbf{x}: h(\mathbf{x})=0\}$ and interior $\operatorname{Int}(\mathcal{C}) = \{\mathbf{x}: h(\mathbf{x})>0\}$.

Definition 1.2 (Forward invariance). The set $\mathcal{C}$ is forward invariant with respect to (1.1) if

\mathbf{x}(0)\in\mathcal{C} \implies \mathbf{x}(t)\in\mathcal{C},\quad \forall t\ge 0.
\tag{1.3}

Definition 1.3 (Safety). System (1.1) is safe with respect to $\mathcal{C}$ if $\mathcal{C}$ is forward invariant.

1.3 The Kinematic Bicycle Model with Curvature Input

For a front-steered vehicle with wheelbase $L$, the kinematic bicycle model in configuration coordinates $\mathbf{q}=[x,y,\theta]^\top$ and speed $v$ is

\dot{x}=v\cos\theta,\quad
\dot{y}=v\sin\theta,\quad
\dot{\theta}=v\kappa,\quad
\dot{v}=a,
\tag{1.4}

where $\kappa=\tan\phi/L$ is the curvature input, $\phi$ is the steering angle, and $a$ is the longitudinal acceleration. The full state is $\mathbf{x}=[x,y,\theta,v]^\top\in\mathcal{X}=\mathbb{R}^2\times\mathbb{S}^1\times\mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}$ and the control is $\mathbf{u}=[a,\kappa]^\top$.

The curvature formulation (1.4) is preferred over the yaw-rate formulation $\dot\theta=\omega$ with $\omega=v\tan\phi/L$ because the recovery map

\phi = \arctan(L\kappa)
\tag{1.5}

is well-defined for all $v\ge 0$, including $v=0$. The physical steering bound $|\phi|\le\phi_{\max}$ becomes the state-independent input bound

|\kappa|\le \kappa_{\max} := \frac{\tan\phi_{\max}}{L}.
\tag{1.6}

Writing $\ddot{\mathbf{p}} = [\ddot x,\ddot y]^\top$ with $\mathbf{p}=[x,y]^\top$, we obtain

\ddot{\mathbf{p}} =
\begin{bmatrix}
a\cos\theta - v^2\kappa\sin\theta\\
a\sin\theta + v^2\kappa\cos\theta
\end{bmatrix},
\tag{1.7}

which is affine in $\mathbf{u}=[a,\kappa]^\top$. This is the structural property that makes the safety filter below a convex quadratic program.

1.4 Model Fidelity: Dynamic Bicycle and Friction Envelope

The kinematic model (1.4) is exact in the low-speed limit but neglects tire slip, lateral load transfer, and actuator dynamics. To retain the dominant higher-order effects while keeping the safety filter tractable, we augment the kinematic model with a friction-envelope barrier derived from the dynamic single-track model.

For a vehicle of mass $m$, yaw inertia $I_z$, and tire-road friction coefficient $\mu$, the lateral and longitudinal accelerations satisfy the friction-circle constraint

a_{\rm lat}^2 + a_{\rm long}^2 \le (\mu g)^2,
\tag{1.8}

where $a_{\rm lat}=v^2\kappa$ and $a_{\rm long}=a$. The conservative convex relaxation

|a| \le \frac{\mu g}{\sqrt{2}},\qquad
|v^2\kappa| \le \frac{\mu g}{\sqrt{2}}
\tag{1.9}

is enforced as a state-dependent input bound within the safety filter. This is a reduced-order model that retains the tire-force limit, and it captures the dominant dynamic constraint beyond the kinematic envelope. The residual model mismatch (slip angles, actuator lag, load transfer) is absorbed into the disturbance bound $\bar d$ of Section 1.1 and propagated formally in Chapter 3.

