Chapter 4 — Recursive Feasibility and the Robust Controllable Subset

4.1 Motivation

Theorem 3.1 guarantees invariance conditional on QP feasibility at every cycle. Classical fallback (brake to stop) is safe only when stopping distance is below $d_{\rm safe}$. To guarantee unconditional safety, we construct a subset of the safe set in which a robust control always exists.

4.2 Robust Controllable Subset

Definition 4.1 (Robust controllable subset). Let $\varepsilon_1,\dots,\varepsilon_r>0$ and define

\Omega = \left\{\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{X} : \psi_i(\mathbf{x})\ge \varepsilon_i,\ i=1,\dots,r\right\}.
\tag{4.1}

The set $\Omega$ is a robust controllable subset of $\bigcap_i\mathcal{C}_i$ if for every $\mathbf{x}\in\Omega$ there exists $\mathbf{u}\in\mathcal{U}$ such that

L_f\psi_{r-1} + L_g\psi_{r-1}\mathbf{u} + \alpha_r(\psi_{r-1}) \ge \varepsilon_r + \iota(\|\mathbf{d}\|_{[0,r-1]}).
\tag{4.2}

Theorem 4.1 (Recursive feasibility). If $\Omega$ is a robust controllable subset and $\mathbf{x}(0)\in\Omega$, then under the HOCBF-QP control the closed-loop trajectory satisfies $\mathbf{x}(t)\in\Omega\subseteq\bigcap_i\mathcal{C}_i$ for all $t\ge 0$, and the QP is feasible at every cycle.

Proof. From (4.2), the HOCBF-QP admits a feasible point at every $\mathbf{x}\in\Omega$. By the HOCBF construction with the tightened margins $\varepsilon_i$, the closed-loop trajectory satisfies $\dot\psi_{i-1}+\alpha_i(\psi_{i-1})\ge 0$ whenever $\psi_i$ approaches $\varepsilon_i$, so $\Omega$ is forward invariant. Induction on the control cycle yields feasibility at every step. $\square$

Corollary 4.1 (Fallback as last resort). The fallback $\mathbf{u}_{\rm backup}=(-a_{\rm dec,max},0)$ is invoked only when $\mathbf{x}\notin\Omega$, which is excluded by Theorem 4.1 under nominal conditions. The fallback is therefore reserved for faults (sensor loss, actuator failure) rather than for routine operation.

4.3 Computation of $\Omega$ and $\varepsilon_i$

In practice $\varepsilon_i$ are chosen via offline reachability analysis or via a self-triggered tightening: $\varepsilon_i$ is set to the maximum value such that the QP remains feasible over a horizon of $N$ cycles under the worst-case disturbance. This yields a distance-to-infeasibility metric used in the experimental protocol (Chapter 10).

