Part II — MOSAIC Architecture

Chapter 6 — Architectural Specification

6.1 Domain Separation

MOSAIC is organized into three domains:

\text{Intelligence} \quad | \quad \text{Safety} \quad | \quad \text{Vehicle}.
\tag{6.1}

The Intelligence domain hosts AI planning and the Autonomy Operating System (AOS) on a high-performance compute module (NVIDIA Jetson Orin). The Safety domain hosts the Safety Kernel on a lock-stepped, ECC-protected microcontroller (Infineon AURIX TC3xx). The Vehicle domain hosts the UVAL and the hardware adapters, colocated with the Safety Kernel on the same MCU.

6.2 Tier Decomposition

Tier Component Domain Rate Certification target
1 Applications Intelligence 1–10 Hz QM
2 AOS Intelligence 10–30 Hz ASIL-B
3 Safety Kernel Safety 100 Hz ASIL-D principles
4 UVAL Vehicle 100 Hz ASIL-D principles
5 Hardware Adapters Vehicle 100 Hz ASIL-D principles

The Safety Kernel and UVAL constitute the deterministic core; the AOS and applications constitute the statistical layer.

6.3 Data Flow and Authority

\text{AI Command} \xrightarrow{\mathbf{u}^{\rm AI}} \text{Safety Kernel} \xrightarrow{\mathbf{u}^*} \text{UVAL} \xrightarrow{\text{Adapter}} \text{Actuator}.
\tag{6.2}

The Safety Kernel is the only component that may write to UVAL. The AI stack never speaks directly to the vehicle. This is the architectural realization of the safety island pattern: a QM-rated high-performance planner is monitored and, if necessary, overridden by an ASIL-D-rated safety kernel.

6.4 Dependent-Failure Analysis

The decomposition assumes independence between the AOS (Tier 2) and the Safety Kernel (Tier 3). Independence is justified by:

1. Physical separation: distinct silicon, distinct power domains, distinct clock sources.
2. Communication isolation: the only inter-tier channel is a unidirectional, CRC-protected CAN-FD link from AOS to Safety Kernel.
3. No shared writable memory: the Safety Kernel maintains its own state estimate from raw sensor inputs.

