Convention A.1. Throughout, $a$ is the signed longitudinal acceleration. Positive $a$ accelerates; negative $a$ decelerates. Emergency braking corresponds to $a=-a_{\rm dec,max}$ with $a_{\rm dec,max}>0$.

Convention A.2. Norms are Euclidean unless otherwise stated: $\|\mathbf{z}\|=\sqrt{\mathbf{z}^\top\mathbf{z}}$.

A.2 Standing Assumptions

Assumption A.1 (Plant regularity). The functions $f:\mathbb{R}^n\to\mathbb{R}^n$, $g:\mathbb{R}^n\to\mathbb{R}^{n\times m}$, $g_d:\mathbb{R}^n\to\mathbb{R}^{n\times p}$ in (1.1) are locally Lipschitz continuous.

Assumption A.2 (Existence and uniqueness). For every $\mathbf{x}(0)\in\mathcal{X}$ and every locally Lipschitz control $\mathbf{u}(\cdot)$, the solution of (1.1) exists and is unique on $[0,T)$.

Assumption A.3 (Bounded disturbance derivatives). The disturbance $d(\cdot)$ is $r{-}1$ times differentiable with $\|d^{(i)}(t)\|\le \bar d_i$ for $i=0,\dots,r{-}1$.

Assumption A.4 (Convex actuator set). The admissible control set $\mathcal{U}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^m$ is convex, compact, and has nonempty interior.

Assumption A.5 (Bounded obstacle acceleration). The obstacle acceleration satisfies $\|\ddot{\mathbf{p}}_o\|\le \bar a_o$.

Assumption A.6 (Ego disturbance bound). The ego disturbance contributes a bounded perturbation $\|\mathbf{d}_{\rm ego}\|\le \bar d'$ to $\ddot{\mathbf{p}}$.

A.3 Class $\mathcal{K}$ and Class $\mathcal{KL}$ Functions

Definition A.1. A continuous function $\alpha:[0,a)\to[0,\infty)$ is class $\mathcal{K}$ if it is strictly increasing and $\alpha(0)=0$. It is class $\mathcal{K}_\infty$ if additionally $a=\infty$ and $\alpha(r)\to\infty$ as $r\to\infty$.

Definition A.2. A continuous function $\beta:[0,a)\times[0,\infty)\to[0,\infty)$ is class $\mathcal{KL}$ if $\beta(\cdot,t)$ is class $\mathcal{K}$ for each fixed $t$, and $\beta(r,\cdot)$ is strictly decreasing to zero for each fixed $r$.

Lemma A.1 (Comparison lemma). Consider the scalar initial-value problem
\dot y = -\alpha(y) + w(t),\qquad y(0)=y_0,


with $\alpha$ class $\mathcal{K}$ and $\|w(t)\|\le \bar w$. If $\alpha^{-1}$ exists on the relevant range, then
y(t)\le \beta(y_0,t) + \alpha^{-1}(\bar w)


for all $t\ge 0$, where $\beta$ is a class $\mathcal{KL}$ function.

