Appendix B — Invariance Proofs for CBF and HOCBF

B.1 Proof of Theorem 2.1 (Standard CBF Invariance)

Theorem 2.1. Let $h$ be a continuously differentiable CBF for (1.1) on $\mathcal{C}$. If the control satisfies
L_f h(\mathbf{x}) + L_g h(\mathbf{x})\mathbf{u} + \alpha(h(\mathbf{x}))\ge 0


for all $\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{C}$, then $\mathcal{C}$ is forward invariant.

Proof. Suppose $\mathbf{x}(0)\in\mathcal{C}$, i.e., $h(\mathbf{x}(0))\ge 0$. We show $h(\mathbf{x}(t))\ge 0$ for all $t\ge 0$ by contradiction.

Assume there exists $t_1>0$ such that $h(\mathbf{x}(t_1))<0$. By continuity of $t\mapsto h(\mathbf{x}(t))$, there exists $t_0\in[0,t_1)$ such that $h(\mathbf{x}(t_0))=0$ and $h(\mathbf{x}(t))<0$ for $t\in(t_0,t_1]$. At $t=t_0$,
\dot h(\mathbf{x}(t_0)) = L_f h + L_g h\,\mathbf{u} \ge -\alpha(h(\mathbf{x}(t_0))) = -\alpha(0) = 0,


where the inequality uses the CBF condition. Hence $\dot h(\mathbf{x}(t_0))\ge 0$, so $h$ is non-decreasing at $t_0$, contradicting $h(\mathbf{x}(t))<0$ immediately after $t_0$. Therefore $h(\mathbf{x}(t))\ge 0$ for all $t\ge 0$, and $\mathcal{C}$ is forward invariant. $\blacksquare$

B.2 Proof of Theorem 2.2 (HOCBF Invariance)

Theorem 2.2. Let $h$ be a valid HOCBF of relative degree $r$ for (1.1) on $\bigcap_{i=1}^{r}\mathcal{C}_i$. If the control satisfies (2.6), then $\bigcap_{i=1}^{r}\mathcal{C}_i$ is forward invariant, provided $\mathbf{x}(0)\in\bigcap_{i=1}^{r}\mathcal{C}_i$.

Proof. Define $\psi_0=h$ and recursively $\psi_i=\dot\psi_{i-1}+\alpha_i(\psi_{i-1})$ for $i=1,\dots,r$. The HOCBF condition (2.6) is
\dot\psi_{r-1}+\alpha_r(\psi_{r-1})\ge 0.

We prove by backward induction that $\psi_i(\mathbf{x}(t))\ge 0$ for all $t\ge 0$ and all $i=0,\dots,r-1$.

Base case ($i=r-1$). The HOCBF condition is exactly $\dot\psi_{r-1}+\alpha_r(\psi_{r-1})\ge 0$. Suppose $\psi_{r-1}(\mathbf{x}(0))\ge 0$. If at some $t_0$ we have $\psi_{r-1}(\mathbf{x}(t_0))=0$, then $\dot\psi_{r-1}\ge -\alpha_r(0)=0$, so $\psi_{r-1}$ is non-decreasing at $t_0$. By the same contradiction argument as in Theorem 2.1, $\psi_{r-1}(\mathbf{x}(t))\ge 0$ for all $t\ge 0$.

Inductive step. Assume $\psi_i(\mathbf{x}(t))\ge 0$ for all $t\ge 0$. Then by definition $\psi_i=\dot\psi_{i-1}+\alpha_i(\psi_{i-1})\ge 0$. Suppose $\psi_{i-1}(\mathbf{x}(0))\ge 0$. If at some $t_0$ we have $\psi_{i-1}(\mathbf{x}(t_0))=0$, then $\dot\psi_{i-1}\ge -\alpha_i(0)=0$, so $\psi_{i-1}$ is non-decreasing at $t_0$. Hence $\psi_{i-1}(\mathbf{x}(t))\ge 0$ for all $t\ge 0$.

By induction, $\psi_i(\mathbf{x}(t))\ge 0$ for all $i=0,\dots,r-1$ and all $t\ge 0$. Since $\mathcal{C}_i=\{\psi_{i-1}\ge 0\}$, we obtain $\mathbf{x}(t)\in\bigcap_{i=1}^{r}\mathcal{C}_i$ for all $t\ge 0$. $\blacksquare$

B.3 Auxiliary Set Structure

Lemma B.1. The HOCBF safe sets satisfy the nesting
\bigcap_{i=1}^{r}\mathcal{C}_i\subseteq\mathcal{C}_1=\mathcal{C}.

Proof. Immediate from $\psi_0=h$ and $\mathcal{C}_1=\{h\ge 0\}$. $\blacksquare$

Remark B.1. The initial condition must satisfy $\mathbf{x}(0)\in\bigcap_{i=1}^{r}\mathcal{C}_i$, which is strictly stronger than $\mathbf{x}(0)\in\mathcal{C}$. In particular, $\psi_1(\mathbf{x}(0))=\dot h(\mathbf{x}(0))+\alpha_1(h(\mathbf{x}(0)))\ge 0$ imposes a constraint on the initial relative velocity.

