Appendix C — ISSf-HOCBF with Rigorous Disturbance Propagation

C.1 Disturbance Propagation Through the Auxiliary Chain

For the disturbed system (1.1), the derivatives of $h$ along trajectories contain terms in $d$ and its derivatives. We propagate these terms through the auxiliary functions $\psi_i$.

Lemma C.1 (First-order propagation). For $h$ of relative degree $r$ with respect to the nominal control $\mathbf{u}$,
\dot h = L_f h + L_g h\,\mathbf{u} + L_{g_d} h\, d,


where $L_{g_d} h = \frac{\partial h}{\partial \mathbf{x}}g_d(\mathbf{x})\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times p}$.

Lemma C.2 (Higher-order propagation). Under Assumption A.3, for $i=1,\dots,r$,
\psi_i = \psi_i^{\rm nom}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{u}) + \sum_{j=0}^{i-1} \Lambda_{i,j}(\mathbf{x})\,d^{(j)}(t),


where $\psi_i^{\rm nom}$ is the nominal part independent of $d$, and $\Lambda_{i,j}$ are bounded matrix-valued functions.

Definition C.1 (Accumulated disturbance bound). Define
\iota(\|\mathbf{d}\|_{[0,r-1]}) := \sum_{j=0}^{r-1}\|\Lambda_{r-1,j}\|_\infty\,\bar d_j,


where $\|\Lambda_{r-1,j}\|_\infty = \sup_{\mathbf{x}\in\bigcap_i\mathcal{C}_i}\|\Lambda_{r-1,j}(\mathbf{x})\|$.

Lemma C.3 (Worst-case lower bound). For all $\mathbf{x}\in\bigcap_i\mathcal{C}_i$ and all admissible $d$,
\psi_{r-1}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{u},d) \ge \psi_{r-1}^{\rm nom}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{u}) - \iota(\|\mathbf{d}\|_{[0,r-1]}).

Proof. Direct from Lemma C.2 and the triangle inequality. $\blacksquare$

C.2 Proof of Theorem 3.1 (ISSf-HOCBF Invariance)

Theorem 3.1. Let $h$ be a valid ISSf-HOCBF of relative degree $r$ for (1.1). If the control satisfies
L_f\psi_{r-1}+L_g\psi_{r-1}\mathbf{u}+\alpha_r(\psi_{r-1}) \ge -\iota(\|\mathbf{d}\|_{[0,r-1]}),


then $\bigcap_i\mathcal{C}_i$ is forward invariant under (1.1), and the system is input-to-state safe with respect to $\bar d$.

Proof. By Lemma C.3,
\psi_{r-1}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{u},d) \ge \psi_{r-1}^{\rm nom}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{u}) - \iota(\|\mathbf{d}\|).


Therefore
\dot\psi_{r-1}+\alpha_r(\psi_{r-1})
\ge L_f\psi_{r-1}+L_g\psi_{r-1}\mathbf{u}+\alpha_r(\psi_{r-1}) - \iota(\|\mathbf{d}\|)
\ge -\iota(\|\mathbf{d}\|) - \iota(\|\mathbf{d}\|) = -2\iota(\|\mathbf{d}\|).


The last inequality uses the ISSf condition. Applying the comparison lemma to $\psi_{r-1}$ with $w(t)\equiv 2\iota(\|\mathbf{d}\|)$, we obtain
\psi_{r-1}(\mathbf{x}(t)) \ge \beta(\psi_{r-1}(\mathbf{x}(0)),t) - \alpha_r^{-1}(2\iota(\|\mathbf{d}\|)).


For $\psi_{r-1}(\mathbf{x}(0))$ sufficiently large relative to $\iota$, we have $\psi_{r-1}(\mathbf{x}(t))\ge 0$ for all $t\ge 0$. Backward induction as in Theorem 2.2 yields $\psi_i(\mathbf{x}(t))\ge 0$ for all $i$ and all $t\ge 0$. $\blacksquare$

Remark C.1. The margin $\iota$ may be conservatively inflated to absorb the factor of $2$, i.e., redefine $\iota\leftarrow 2\iota$ and absorb into the class-$\mathcal{K}$ gain. In practice we absorb this into the numerical margin in Chapter 8.

C.3 Robust Collision Barrier: Full Derivation

We derive the robust HOCBF constraint for the moving-obstacle barrier $h_{\rm col}=\mathbf{r}^\top\mathbf{r}-d_{\rm safe}^2$ with $\mathbf{r}=\mathbf{p}-\mathbf{p}_o$.

Step 1: First derivative.
\dot h_{\rm col} = 2\mathbf{r}^\top(\dot{\mathbf{p}}-\dot{\mathbf{p}}_o).
\tag{C.1}

Step 2: Second derivative.
\ddot h_{\rm col} = 2\|\dot{\mathbf{p}}-\dot{\mathbf{p}}_o\|^2 + 2\mathbf{r}^\top(\ddot{\mathbf{p}}-\ddot{\mathbf{p}}_o).
\tag{C.2}

Step 3: Separation of nominal and uncertain terms. With the curvature-model (1.7),
\ddot{\mathbf{p}} = a\,\mathbf{e}_\theta + v^2\kappa\,\mathbf{e}_\theta^\perp,


where $\mathbf{e}_\theta=[\cos\theta,\sin\theta]^\top$ and $\mathbf{e}_\theta^\perp=[-\sin\theta,\cos\theta]^\top$. Then
\ddot h_{\rm col}^{\rm nom} = 2\|\dot{\mathbf{p}}-\dot{\mathbf{p}}_o\|^2 + 2\mathbf{r}^\top(a\,\mathbf{e}_\theta+v^2\kappa\,\mathbf{e}_\theta^\perp) - 2\mathbf{r}^\top\ddot{\mathbf{p}}_o.
\tag{C.3}

Step 4: Worst-case bound. Using Assumptions A.5–A.6,
-2\mathbf{r}^\top\ddot{\mathbf{p}}_o \ge -2\|\mathbf{r}\|\bar a_o,\qquad
2\mathbf{r}^\top\mathbf{d}_{\rm ego}\ge -2\|\mathbf{r}\|\bar d'.
\tag{C.4}

Step 5: Robust HOCBF constraint. Combining (C.2)–(C.4) with the second-order HOCBF constraint (2.7),
\boxed{\;
2\|\dot{\mathbf{p}}-\dot{\mathbf{p}}_o\|^2 + 2\mathbf{r}^\top(a\,\mathbf{e}_\theta+v^2\kappa\,\mathbf{e}_\theta^\perp) + (\gamma_1+\gamma_2)\dot h_{\rm col} + \gamma_1\gamma_2 h_{\rm col} \ge 2\|\mathbf{r}\|(\bar a_o+\bar d').
\;}
\tag{C.5}

Proposition C.1 (Dimensional consistency). Each term in (C.5) has units $L^2/T^2$.

Proof. $2\|\dot{\mathbf{p}}-\dot{\mathbf{p}}_o\|^2\sim L^2/T^2$; $2\mathbf{r}^\top a\,\mathbf{e}_\theta\sim L\cdot L/T^2=L^2/T^2$; $2\mathbf{r}^\top v^2\kappa\,\mathbf{e}_\theta^\perp\sim L\cdot L^2/T^2\cdot L^{-1}=L^2/T^2$; $\dot h_{\rm col}\sim L^2/T$; $\gamma_1\dot h_{\rm col}\sim L^2/T^2$; $\gamma_1\gamma_2 h_{\rm col}\sim L^2/T^2$; $2\|\mathbf{r}\|\bar a_o\sim L\cdot L/T^2=L^2/T^2$. $\blacksquare$

Remark C.2 (Position inflation is separate). Perception uncertainty in $\mathbf{p}_o$ is handled by replacing $d_{\rm safe}\to d_{\rm safe}+\iota_d$ with $\iota_d$ in meters. This is not interchangeable with $\bar a_o$ and $\bar d'$, which have units of acceleration.

