Appendix D — Recursive Feasibility and the Robust Controllable Subset

D.1 Construction of $\Omega$

Definition D.1 (Robust controllable subset). Let $\varepsilon_1,\dots,\varepsilon_r>0$ and define
\Omega = \left\{\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{X} : \psi_i(\mathbf{x})\ge \varepsilon_i,\ i=1,\dots,r\right\}.

Definition D.2 (Robust controllability). The set $\Omega$ is robustly controllable if for every $\mathbf{x}\in\Omega$ there exists $\mathbf{u}\in\mathcal{U}$ such that
L_f\psi_{r-1}+L_g\psi_{r-1}\mathbf{u}+\alpha_r(\psi_{r-1}) \ge \varepsilon_r+\iota(\|\mathbf{d}\|).
\tag{D.1}

Lemma D.1 (Monotonicity in $\varepsilon$). If $\Omega$ is robustly controllable for $\varepsilon=(\varepsilon_1,\dots,\varepsilon_r)$, then any $\Omega'$ with $\varepsilon_i'\le\varepsilon_i$ is also robustly controllable.

Proof. The condition (D.1) is easier to satisfy for smaller $\varepsilon_r$. $\blacksquare$

Lemma D.2 (Existence of $\varepsilon$). Under Assumptions A.4–A.6, if there exists $\mathbf{x}^\star\in\operatorname{Int}(\bigcap_i\mathcal{C}_i)$ such that (D.1) holds with strict inequality at $\varepsilon=0$, then there exists $\varepsilon>0$ such that $\Omega\ne\emptyset$ and robustly controllable.

Proof. By continuity of $\psi_i$ and the affine structure of (D.1) in $\mathbf{u}$, strict feasibility at $\mathbf{x}^\star$ implies a neighborhood on which (D.1) holds with margin. Choose $\varepsilon$ smaller than the minimum margin on that neighborhood. $\blacksquare$

D.2 Proof of Theorem 4.1 (Recursive Feasibility)

Theorem 4.1. If $\Omega$ is a robust controllable subset and $\mathbf{x}(0)\in\Omega$, then under the HOCBF-QP control the closed-loop trajectory satisfies $\mathbf{x}(t)\in\Omega$ for all $t\ge 0$, and the QP is feasible at every cycle.

Proof. We prove by induction on the control cycle index $k$. Let $\Delta t$ be the control period.

Base case. $\mathbf{x}_0=\mathbf{x}(0)\in\Omega$, so the QP is feasible by Definition D.2.

Inductive step. Assume $\mathbf{x}_k\in\Omega$ and the QP is feasible. Let $\mathbf{u}_k^\star$ be the QP solution. By (D.1), $\mathbf{u}_k^\star$ satisfies
\dot\psi_{r-1}+\alpha_r(\psi_{r-1}) \ge \varepsilon_r+\iota(\|\mathbf{d}\|) - \iota(\|\mathbf{d}\|) = \varepsilon_r > 0


along the disturbed trajectory. Applying the comparison lemma, $\psi_{r-1}(\mathbf{x}_{k+1})\ge \varepsilon_r$ for the next sample. By backward induction as in Theorem 2.2, $\psi_i(\mathbf{x}_{k+1})\ge\varepsilon_i$ for all $i$. Hence $\mathbf{x}_{k+1}\in\Omega$, and the QP is feasible at step $k+1$.

By induction, $\mathbf{x}_k\in\Omega$ for all $k\ge 0$, and the QP is feasible at every cycle. Since $\Omega\subseteq\bigcap_i\mathcal{C}_i$, forward invariance of $\bigcap_i\mathcal{C}_i$ follows. $\blacksquare$

D.3 Computation of $\varepsilon_i$ via Reachability

Algorithm D.1 (Offline computation of $\varepsilon$).

1. Discretize the state space over the operational envelope.
2. For each grid point, solve the QP (5.4) with $\varepsilon=0$.
3. Record the minimal slack $\sigma(\mathbf{x})$ over all constraints.
4. Set $\varepsilon_i = \min_{\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{X}_{\rm op}}\sigma_i(\mathbf{x}) - \delta$, with $\delta>0$ a safety margin.
5. Verify $\Omega\ne\emptyset$; if empty, reduce $\delta$ or expand $\mathcal{X}_{\rm op}$.

Remark D.1. The distance-to-infeasibility metric used in Chapter 10 is
d_{\rm inf}(\mathbf{x}) = \min_i \left\{\psi_i(\mathbf{x}) - \varepsilon_i\right\}.


Positive $d_{\rm inf}$ certifies that $\mathbf{x}\in\Omega$ and the QP is feasible.

