Appendix K — Extended Falsifiability and Portability Criteria

K.1 Quantitative Falsifiability

Definition K.1 (Robust feasibility rate). Over $N$ cycles,
\rho_{\rm feas} = \frac{1}{N}\sum_{k=1}^{N}\mathbf{1}\{d_{\rm inf}(\mathbf{x}_k)>0\}.

Criterion K.1. The thesis is falsified if $\rho_{\rm feas}<99\%$ under sensor noise.

Definition K.2 (Certificate validity rate). Over $N$ cycles,
\rho_{\rm cert} = \frac{1}{N}\sum_{k=1}^{N}\mathbf{1}\{\text{KKT residual}<10^{-4}\}.

Criterion K.2. The thesis is falsified if $\rho_{\rm cert}<99.9\%$.

K.2 Portability Criterion

Definition K.3 (Portability). The architecture is portable if a second vehicle can be integrated within three months by:

1. Implementing a new adapter satisfying the UVAL contract.
2. Calibrating $(L,m,\tau_{\max},a_{\rm dec,max},a_{\rm acc,max},\kappa_{\max},\mu,Q_{\rm nom},V_{\rm nom})$.
3. Producing no modification to the Safety Kernel or HOCBF-QP formulation.

Criterion K.3. The thesis is falsified if portability fails on the second vehicle.

K.3 Statistical Power

For each falsifiability criterion, the experimental protocol is designed with power $1-\beta\ge 0.9$ at significance level $\alpha=0.01$. Sample sizes are computed from pilot data on the Dacia Spring.

