Appendix M — Open Problems and Future Work

1. Formal proof of $N_{\rm it}\le 3$. A rigorous proof requires strict complementarity and Lipschitz continuity of the QP data. Currently verified numerically; a formal proof is future work.
2. Perception-aware barriers. Replacing the deterministic $h_{\rm col}$ with a probabilistic barrier incorporating track covariance is an active research direction.
3. Multi-agent interaction. The current architecture treats other agents as bounded-acceleration disturbances. Incorporating a simple interactive model (e.g., responsibility-sensitive safety) is future work.
4. Dynamic bicycle extension. The friction-envelope barrier (3.12) is a reduced-order approximation. A full dynamic-bicycle HOCBF with relative degree $r\ge 3$ is a natural extension.
5. Certified solver qualification. The fixed-iteration solver is designed to ASIL-D principles but requires toolchain and qualification evidence for a full ASIL-D claim.
6. Homologation beyond Morocco. Extension to EU (UNECE R157) and US (NHTSA) frameworks is future work.

